    "research_current_explanation": "The zero-accuracy public record provides no evidence that learned absolute positional vectors help addition. They may spend capacity memorizing positions rather than encoding reusable digit distances.",
    "research_evidence": "The current visible record executed successfully and was eligible, but public accuracy was 0.0. Its result does not isolate or support learned positional parameters.",
    "research_next_experiment": "Replace only the learned positional mechanism with a parameter-free sinusoidal mechanism while preserving width, attention topology, residual algebra, normalization, and tied readout.",
    "research_expected_result": "The graph should remain eligible with fewer trusted parameters, and stable positional phases may produce non-worse or improved public accuracy.",
    "research_decision_rule": "Retain the sinusoidal mechanism if eligibility is preserved and trusted parameter count decreases; apply public accuracy only when comparing architectures with equal trusted size.",
    "research_previous_interpretation": "The previous direction removed unsupported feed-forward parameters, but its evidence did not establish that learned positional parameters were necessary.",
    "research_previous_changed": "yes",
    "research_challenged_assumption": "Learned absolute positional embeddings are needed to represent digit alignment and carry distance in this short autoregressive task.",
    "research_alternative_explanation": "Fixed sinusoidal phases may provide sufficient ordinal and distance information while avoiding position-specific parameters that receive sparse or unstable training signals.",
    "research_discriminating_evidence": "Preserved eligibility with lower parameter count and non-worse accuracy would favor fixed positions; loss of eligibility or a decisive accuracy decrease would favor learned positional embeddings."
